    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **No, you should not make an issue of the panel being made up entirely of women.** It is very unlikely to be a sign of systemic bias against you, nor does it provide grounds for a formal challenge.\n\nHere is why, along with what you should focus on instead:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the Panel's Gender Composition Is Not a Factor\n* **Both you and the selected candidate are women:** Because the successful candidate shares your gender, an all-female panel does not point to unlawful sex or gender discrimination.\n* **Common in education:** In many school districts and educational authorities, senior leadership panels, HR representatives, and superintendent teams happen to be predominantly or entirely female simply due to workforce demographics.\n* **Risk of undermining your credibility:** Raising the gender composition of the panel in an appeal or complaint will likely distract from any legitimate procedural questions and may make your inquiry appear unfocused or reactionary.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What Might Actually Explain the Decision\nWhile your paper credentials in administration (a Master’s in Educational Leadership + 5 years as deputy principal) appear stronger on the leadership side than the appointed candidate’s profile:\n* **Classroom experience vs. administrative experience:** The panel may have prioritized extensive frontline teaching experience (10 years) over formal administrative credentials.\n* **Interview performance and institutional fit:** Once candidates reach the final interview stage, decisions often hinge heavily on:\n  * How candidates answered scenario-based questions.\n  * Alignment with the school board’s or community's current strategic vision (e.g., student wellbeing, pedagogical reform, staff culture).\n  * Reference checks and track record with specific school initiatives.\n* **Internal candidate status:** It is very common for an internal candidate or someone already known to the district/panel to have an advantage regarding perceived cultural fit.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Do About the Two-Week Silence\nA two-week wait for formal post-interview feedback or a written rationale is common in education administration:\n* Human resources and legal teams often review written explanations before they are released to ensure compliance with hiring regulations.\n* The formal appointment contract may still be finalizing.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n1. **Send a professional follow-up:**  \n   Politely follow up on your request for written feedback, framing it around professional growth:\n   > *\"I am following up on my request from two weeks ago regarding the written summary/feedback from the recent principal selection process. As I look to continue my professional development in school leadership, I would greatly appreciate any insights the panel can share regarding areas of strength and opportunities for improvement.\"*\n2. **Review local hiring and public sector transparency rules:**  \n   Depending on your jurisdiction (e.g., public vs. independent school system, collective bargaining agreements, state/provincial public service rules), applicants often have a legal right to view the anonymized scoring matrix or formal qualification assessment.\n3. **Keep the focus on objective criteria:**  \n   If you believe the process violated specific selection rules (e.g., minimum mandatory qualifications set out in the job advertisement), focus strictly on **job criteria, scoring, and process adherence**, not demographic factors.\n",
